  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Time-Domain vs. Frequency-Domain Views",
    "lesson_core_move": "Translate a periodic signal between its time-domain waveform and frequency-domain harmonic description by identifying how DC, fundamental frequency, harmonic frequencies, and sine/cosine basis components encode the same signal.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Domain vs. Frequency-Domain Views",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time-Domain vs. Frequency-Domain Views",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A periodic signal can be read in two equivalent ways: as a time-domain waveform and as a frequency-domain harmonic description. The signal does not change; only the representation changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the time-domain view, you look at $x(t)$ as a waveform across one period. In the frequency-domain view, you list the DC term and the harmonic components at integer multiples of $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the simplest base case: one DC term and one fundamental harmonic. That base case lets you match the constant offset to the zero-frequency term and the oscillating part to the first harmonic without extra algebra.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=a_0+a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The shared landmarks are $a_0$, $\\omega_0$, and $k\\omega_0$: $a_0$ is the DC component, $\\omega_0$ sets the spacing between harmonics, and $k\\omega_0$ identifies the $k$th harmonic. The sine and cosine terms are the basis pieces that encode the same periodic signal in frequency form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=a_0+\\sum_{k=1}^{N}\\big[a_k\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)+b_k\\sin(k\\omega_0 t)\\big]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the later sections as translations between these views. Each time you move from waveform to harmonic list, you are describing the same signal in a different language, not introducing a different signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Domain vs. Frequency-Domain Views",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Reading DC and Fundamental Frequency Across Views",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal has $x(t)=3+2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$. Identify its DC term and fundamental angular frequency, then state how those show up in the frequency-domain description.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the constant term in $x(t)=3+2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$. A constant offset does not oscillate, so it is the DC term, and here $a_0=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The oscillating part is the cosine at angular frequency $\\omega_0$. That makes $\\omega_0$ the fundamental angular frequency, the first harmonic spacing in the frequency-domain view.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the same signal as a frequency-domain description with a zero-frequency term and a harmonic at $\\omega_0$. The constant part becomes the DC component, and the cosine part becomes the first harmonic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the frequency-domain side says: DC term $a_0=3$ and fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0$. The amplitude 2 is the weight on that fundamental cosine component.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the key translation: the waveform's baseline and first oscillation are not separate ideas, just the same signal read in two representations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Domain vs. Frequency-Domain Views",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Reading Harmonic Frequencies Across Views",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal is given by $x(t)=1+4\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-2\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)$. Which harmonic frequencies appear in its frequency-domain description, and which term corresponds to each frequency?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the frequency inside each sinusoid, not the size of its coefficient. The constant term $1$ is the DC component, so $a_0=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The term $4\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ is the first harmonic, because its angular frequency is $\\omega_0$. The term $-2\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)$ is the third harmonic, because its angular frequency is $3\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=1+4\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-2\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\{0,\\omega_0,3\\omega_0\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the frequency-domain description contains a zero-frequency term, a line at $\\omega_0$, and a line at $3\\omega_0$. The coefficient values 4 and -2 change the weights on those lines, not the harmonic locations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "There is no $2\\omega_0$ line, because no term in the waveform uses that harmonic. Reading the frequency-domain view is just naming the frequencies actually present in the time-domain sum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Domain vs. Frequency-Domain Views",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Matching Sine and Cosine Basis Components Across Views",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal is described by the frequency-domain coefficients $a_1=2$, $b_1=-1$, and $a_2=3$ with $a_0=0$. Write the equivalent time-domain Fourier-series form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the coefficient label attached to each harmonic, not the waveform shape. Since $a_0=0$, there is no DC term. The coefficients $a_k$ belong to cosine terms and the coefficients $b_k$ belong to sine terms at the same harmonic index.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $a_1=2$ becomes $2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$, $b_1=-1$ becomes $-\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$, and $a_2=3$ becomes $3\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)$. The sign stays with the coefficient, and the harmonic index stays on the angle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_0=0,\\quad a_1=2,\\quad b_1=-1,\\quad a_2=3 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; x(t)=2\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+3\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The assembled time-domain form uses the same harmonic content in basis-function form: first harmonic cosine, first harmonic sine, and second harmonic cosine. Nothing changes except the representation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the matching rule students should reuse: read the coefficient label, choose cosine or sine, place it at the correct $k\\omega_0$, and preserve the sign. That reconstructs the time-domain Fourier-series expression directly from the frequency-domain coefficients.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Domain vs. Frequency-Domain Views",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Translating a Full Periodic Signal Description Across Views",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal is given by $x(t)=2+3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)$. State the matching frequency-domain harmonic description and then read the same signal back from that description.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is to read each coefficient together with its harmonic and basis type. In $x(t)=2+3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)$, the constant term is the DC component, the $\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ and $\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$ terms belong to the first harmonic, and the $\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)$ term belongs to the second harmonic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2+3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; a_0=2,\\quad a_1=3,\\quad b_1=-1,\\quad a_2=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the frequency-domain reading keeps the same information but names it by coefficient labels: DC is $a_0=2$, first-harmonic cosine is $a_1=3$, first-harmonic sine is $b_1=-1$, and second-harmonic cosine is $a_2=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_0=2,\\quad a_1=3,\\quad b_1=-1,\\quad a_2=4\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; x(t)=2+3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is a one-to-one translation: preserve the DC term, preserve the harmonic index, choose sine or cosine correctly, and keep the sign on the coefficient. That is the full cross-view match the student should imitate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
